<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nafplio</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nauplia, Nauplion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nafplio, historically Nauplia, is a port town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, overlooked by the fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acronauplia">Acronauplia</a> and Palamidi. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-sgouros">Leo Sgouros</a>, then by Frankish lords and from 1388 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which made it a principal base in the Morea. It was the first capital of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, until the government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 1834.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nafplio.jpg" alt="Nafplio" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%94%CE%B7%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B5%CE%AF%CE%BF_%CE%9D%CE%B1%CF%85%CF%80%CE%BB%CE%AF%CE%BF%CF%85,_Nafplio_Town_Hall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
